{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UknYrdC4DXy26w7gjjvdsP","type":"tool_result","content":"VERSION ?= $(shell git describe --tags --always --dirty 2>/dev/null || echo dev)\nLDFLAGS := -s -w -X main.version=$(VERSION)\n\n.PHONY: build test lint fmt clean cve-fixtures lint-scanner-fixtures chart-test image-test\n\nbuild:\n\tCGO_ENABLED=0 go build -ldflags \"$(LDFLAGS)\" -o bin/ard ./cmd/ard\n\ntest:\n\tgo test ./...\n\nlint:\n\tgo vet ./...\n\t@out=\"$$(gofmt -l .)\"; if [ -n \"$$out\" ]; then echo \"gofmt needed:\"; echo \"$$out\"; exit 1; fi\n\nfmt:\n\tgofmt -w .\n\n# Renders the two Argo CD charts under deploy/ and checks them (needs helm and yq;\n# run by the ard-ci pipeline, not part of `make test`).\nchart-test:\n\tdeploy/test-charts.sh\n\n# Builds the ard image for both platforms and checks it (needs Docker buildx and\n# network; not part of `make test`).\nimage-test:\n\tdeploy/test-image.sh\n\n# Regenerates the real cve scanner fixtures inside the pinned tool image.\n# Needs Docker and network; not part of `make test`.\ncve-fixtures:\n\tdocker build -t ard-cve-fixtures internal/roles/scanners/cve\n\tdocker run --rm --user \"$$(id -u):$$(id -g)\" -e HOME=/tmp \\\n\t  -v \"$$PWD/internal/roles:/roles\" -w /roles ard-cve-fixtures sh testdata/cve/regen.sh\n\n# Regenerates the real lint scanner fixtures in the pinned image (see\n# internal/roles/testdata/lint/regen.sh). Needs Docker; not part of `make test`.\nlint-scanner-fixtures:\n\tdocker build -t ard-lint-fixtures internal/roles/scanners/lint\n\tdocker run --rm --user \"$$(id -u):$$(id -g)\" -e HOME=/tmp \\\n\t  -v \"$$PWD/internal/roles:/roles\" -w /roles ard-lint-fixtures sh testdata/lint/regen.sh\n\nclean:\n\trm -rf bin\ncmd/ard:\nmain.go\nmain_test.go\n\ndocs:\nTODO.md\nlayout.md\nproposal.md\nrunbooks\n\ninternal:\nagent\nagentjob\nbotcheck\nbudget\ncli\ncloseout\ncommitter\nconfig\nconfigcmd\nconfigsrc\ncontroller\ncostblock\ncosts\neffects\nexecutor\ngithub\ngitref\ngitx\ninitcmd\nintake\njanitor\nlabels\nlimits\nmerger\nmodelauth\npool\nprgate\nreaper\nredact\nrepopath\nreviewer\nreviewfix\nroles\nscanner\nservecmd\nstate\ntriage\nworker\nwtlock\n# ard configuration for albertcbrown/ard (dogfooding). Started from `ard init`'s\n# template; human-owned. ard serve reads it from origin/main.\n# Anything unset takes its built-in default (docs/proposal.md, Configuration).\n# Validate with `ard config check`. ard serve reads this file from origin/main.\n\n# label: only issues a maintainer labels for triage enter the pipeline.\n# all: every human-filed issue enters.\nintake: { mode: label }\n\nmerge:\n  # A PR touching any of these waits for a human approval. There is no\n  # built-in list: removing an entry removes its gate. An entry ending in /\n  # is a directory prefix; anything else is a glob (quote globs starting with *).\n  protected_paths:\n    - .github/\n    - .ard/config.yaml\n    - .ard/scanners/\n    - .ard/triage/\n    - .ard/workers/\n    - .ard/reviewers/\n    - \"**/CODEOWNERS\"\n    # Agent instructions a PR could plant to steer later agents.\n    - CLAUDE.md\n    - \"**/CLAUDE.md\"\n    - AGENTS.md\n    - \"**/AGENTS.md\"\n    - .claude/\n    - .mcp.json\n    # What builds, signs and deploys ard itself (docs/proposal.md, Delivery).\n    - \"Jenkinsfile*\"\n    - Dockerfile\n    - .dockerignore\n    - deploy/\n\noverrides:\n  scanners/cve: { every: 6h, max_open: 10, group_size: 1, autoclose: true }\n  # Phase 1 agent roles (the ard init starter set, #39 Gate 2).\n  triage/lead:  { model: claude-opus-5-5, timeout: 15m }\n  triage/engineer: { model: claude-sonnet-5-5 }\n  workers/engineer: { model: claude-sonnet-5-5, timeout: 60m, tests: make test }\n  reviewers/eng-review: { model: claude-sonnet-5-5 }\n89:**Claims need a single owner, not atomic labels.** GitHub labels are not a lock: two pollers can both add `:running`. Phase 1 runs one controller per repo behind a host lock (`flock` locally, a Kubernetes Lease in-cluster), so the controller is the only claimer. If you ever run several, the claimer re-reads the issue's label timeline and backs off unless its own `labeled` event is first.\n256:The starter engineer (`.ard/triage/engineer/prompt.txt`) plans for the worker without implementing. Its inputs are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md`, `comments.md` and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`, all untrusted. It writes the `## Implementation` section of `plan.md` with Steps, Test plan, Risk and Size, sized S, M, L or XL on a scale tied to the worker diff guard's block limit (XL is over the worker role's `diff_max_lines`, 2,000 changed lines by default). It writes `triage-engineer.md` with `role: engineer` and `attempt: 1`, never a `worker` key. It answers `needs-human` for an XL change, a change to protected role and config paths or `.github/`, or a missing product or design decision, and leaves `plan.md` untouched. In Phase 1 it has no `after:`; the lead's request orders it. The committer and close-out guards confine it to `.ard/<issue#>/`, and close-out allows it only `triage-engineer.md` and the `## Implementation` section of `plan.md`; a change to `triage-lead.md`, `issue.md` or another role's section fails the job as needs-human.\n262:Before it asks for approval or starts a worker, the gate checks that `.ard/<n>/plan.md` exists on `main`. If it does not, the gate posts one comment, adds `ard:needs-human` and records a `gate` event with reason `no-plan`; a check that fails to run is retried on the next tick. Removing a role's request label by hand does not count as that role finishing. With `require_approval: true` (the Phase 1 default) the gate posts one \"ready for approval\" comment per lead job and waits. `ard:approved` is honoured only when its latest `labeled` event (reset by an `unlabeled`) is by a collaborator with triage permission or higher, is not the App, and comes after the last triage `:done` label of the issue. An approval given earlier is left in place and ignored: the gate waits for a fresh one and does not escalate. An approval from anyone else adds `ard:needs-human` with a comment that does not name the actor. A human who adds `ard:worker:<role>` directly bypasses the gate and has approved by doing so. The gate does not enforce that `triage:engineer` ran: the lead routes and the gate applies its routing. In `--dry-run` the gate logs `triage.would_gate` and writes nothing; otherwise each action records a `gate` event.\n283:Local-executor details: the role directory (and its `Dockerfile`) is mounted from the config snapshot at the job's SHA, not from the working tree. The job worktree is always detached at a SHA resolved from the job's ref (the executor never checks out a branch; the committer owns branch worktrees). The controller's clone is a bare repository ard owns at `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` (lowercased), with no remote, no stored credential and auto-gc off (`gc.auto=0`, `maintenance.auto=false`). `ard serve` creates it on first start and seeds it with one fetch of `main` (up to 30 min; if that fails, serve starts paused and the next start retries), and later starts reuse it. Config loads and committer lanes fetch into it, and every committer and executor worktree is cut from it (lanes under `~/.ard/work/<owner>/<repo>/lanes/`). `ard serve` never writes to the operator's checkout: it reads it only for the `origin` fallback of `--repo` and the local `paused` key. To rebuild a damaged clone, stop serve, `rm -rf` it and restart. Upgrading from a version that used the checkout: ard deletes its old lane and job worktree directories without running git there, so the old `refs/ard/*` refs and the dangling worktree registrations stay in the checkout until you remove them (`git for-each-ref --format='delete %(refname)' refs/ard/ | git update-ref --stdin; git worktree prune`). Every `git worktree add`, `remove` and `prune` on the controller's clone, by the executor and the committer's lanes alike, takes one in-process lock keyed by the clone's common git directory, because git's worktree bookkeeping is not safe under concurrent admin commands; fetches, commits and pushes do not take it. Each such command also has a 30 s deadline, and cleanup runs even when the job's context is cancelled, so one hung git cannot stall every lane. Do not run `git worktree prune` (or add/remove) in `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` by hand while `ard serve` runs: the lock is per process and cannot cover a human's git. The operator's own checkout is free to use. The host keeps two files per job: `<base>.log` (stdout and stderr combined, the heartbeat source and the committed log) and `<base>.stdout` (stdout only, parsed by the adapter). A read-only job (`ReadOnly`, used by scanners) gets the worktree mounted `:ro` under Docker, or `chmod -R a-w` on the host. The heartbeat is the log file's mtime while the PID is alive; liveness alone is not a beat. Phase 0 local jobs do not survive a controller restart: the pipes belong to the controller, so after a restart the job reports `orphaned` (PID alive, start time verified) or `gone`, and the Reaper kills and fails it. The executor is not an effect: it never calls GitHub or commits, so jobs run normally under `--dry-run`. Sidecar auth: the sidecar fetch runs in the controller with the GitHub App installation token in a host-scoped `http.<url>.extraheader` passed through git's environment, never argv; the job receives plain files and no credential, and the cache stores no remote and no token. The App must be installed on the sidecar repo with contents:read. Host-mode jobs can reach `~/.ard/sidecars`, but it holds no credential. Host-mode jobs run as the controller's uid: they can read the GitHub App key and `~/.config/gh` by absolute path, write the controller's clone, and undo `chmod a-w`. Host mode is allowed for every role ([#84](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/84)), but a role that reads untrusted input (every triage, worker and reviewer role, and an LLM scanner) warns loudly when it has no `Dockerfile`; deterministic scanners are exempt, and nothing warns under `executor: k8s`, where every job runs in a container. `ard config check` and the first successful config load of `ard serve` (and any role a later config adds) log one `executor.host_untrusted` WARN per such role and also write it to stderr, so it survives `--log-level`. The local executor logs the WARN again for every host-mode job of such a role and appends a `host_untrusted` row to the `events` table. Each warning lists what the job can reach, including `~/.ard` (state, config snapshots, the pause file, other jobs' worktrees and logs). The owner accepts this risk while ard runs only on owner-controlled repos, and revisits it when moving to the spark cluster, where every job runs in a container once the k8s executor (#54) lands. Until then the Gate 1 dry-run soak on spark (see Rollout and Delivery) runs `ard serve` with the local executor in a pod, and the deterministic cve scanner runs there as a host subprocess of the controller container: its role directory has no `Dockerfile`, and the `ard` image carries its pinned tools. Only deterministic scanners run in that pod: it starts `ard serve --loops scanners,stale,reaper` ([#187](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/187)), so the triage, worker and reviewer loops never run there, even though `.ard/config.yaml` on `main` has agent roles. `--loops` takes a comma-separated list of loop names (scanners, stale, triage, workers, reviewers, reviewfix, merger, janitor, reaper, budget; `budget` is the status-issue notice loop, the claim gate is always on), defaults to every loop, and rejects an unknown name with exit 2. `ard serve` and `ard config check` refuse to run on git older than 2.47.0, because the committer's fetches fail on older versions.\n291:| Workspace | `git worktree` of the controller's clone (`~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git`) under `~/.ard/work/<owner>/<repo>/<job-id>`; sidecar exported into the job home | `Job` with an init container that shallow-clones the branch into an `emptyDir`; sidecar cloned by an init container (Phase 3) |\n296:| Good for | Phase 1, one host, fast iteration | parallelism, isolation, untrusted inputs |\n347:5. **Close-out.** The controller posts one PR review at the pinned commit (event `COMMENT`): findings on lines the PR added are inline comments, every other finding is listed in the review body. If GitHub answers 422 (a line or the commit is not in the PR), it posts the review again with every finding in the body, and if that is also refused, as a plain PR comment. It commits `review-<role>.md` and the run's redacted output to the PR branch through the per-branch committer (no `[skip ci]`: PR-branch commits always run CI so required checks report on the head), and swaps `:running` for `:done` or `:changes`. The reviewer never edits the PR. After a close-out (and on every tick, so a restart cannot miss it) the controller marks a draft worker PR ready for review once every requested review is `:done` at the PR's current head and no `:changes`, `:running`, `ard:executor:failed`, `ard:needs-human`, `ard:hold` or `ard:tests:failed` label is present; it never converts a PR back to draft and never merges, which stays with the merger (Phase 2) and humans.\n462:- **Identity: a GitHub App, not a PAT.** Installation tokens expire hourly and are scoped to contents, issues and pull requests (read and write), plus checks (read and write) and commit statuses (read). The merger reads check runs (checks: read), commit statuses (commit statuses: read) and the branch rules (metadata). The merger needs read to see required checks; the owner kept write so ard can later publish its own check runs, but nothing in Phase 1 writes checks. Leaving out the `workflows` permission makes GitHub itself reject any push that edits `.github/workflows/`, a second backstop behind a guard that already refuses all of `.github/`, and it also keeps the bot from editing the bot-commit audit. The scratch-repo run (#85) confirmed that GitHub rejects such a push (\"refusing to allow a GitHub App to create or update workflow ... without `workflows` permission\"), and that these work with exactly this permission set: label writes, PR create, review and mark-ready, pinning the status issue, and timeline and collaborator-permission reads. A personal token (`ARD_GITHUB_TOKEN`) is accepted only for local `--dry-run` development, and the client is then made read-only so no write can reach GitHub.\n509:## Rollout\n515:    P0[\"Phase 0: Skeleton<br/>controller and config<br/>label bootstrap<br/>committer, local executor<br/>cve and stale scanners<br/>dry-run mode\"] -->|\"Gate 1: 1 week dry-run, 0 duplicate issues\"| P1[\"Phase 1: First loop<br/>lead + engineer triage<br/>engineer worker<br/>eng-review<br/>Reaper<br/>human approval gate\"]\n516:    P1 -->|\"Gate 2: 10 merged PRs, failures under 10%\"| P2[\"Phase 2: All roles<br/>all triage roles<br/>all four reviewers<br/>review fix rounds<br/>merger loop<br/>remaining scanners\"]\n517:    P2 -->|\"Gate 3: 50% merged with no human code edits\"| P3[\"Phase 3: Scale<br/>k8s executor<br/>second repo<br/>shared roles repo<br/>budgets and digest\"]\n518:    P3 --> P4[\"Phase 4: Docs and site<br/>user documentation<br/>GitHub Pages site\"]\n519:    P4 --> P5[\"Phase 5: Distribution<br/>versioned releases<br/>Homebrew cask\"]\n522:Phases 4 and 5 have no gate: Phase 4 ([#178](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/178)) writes the user documentation and publishes it as a GitHub Pages site, and Phase 5 ([#177](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/177)) publishes versioned releases and a Homebrew cask (`brew install --cask albertcbrown/tap/ard`).\n524:**Where it runs (decided, #5; amended for the soak, [#144](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/144)).** `ard serve` runs live on the owner's Mac with the local executor until Phase 3, when it moves to the \"spark\" Kubernetes cluster with the k8s executor and a Lease lock in place of the `flock`. The Gate 1 soak week does not run on the Mac: it runs on spark as `ard serve --dry-run` (one pod, `Recreate`, state on a persistent volume), and the week counts from the first healthy pod; no other `ard serve` for the same repo runs meanwhile, so one ledger is counted. See Delivery. ard supports macOS and Linux only; Windows is unsupported.\n526:Phase 0 runs in dry-run: the controller logs every issue, label and commit it would make, and does none of them. Dry-run keeps its own state (`~/.ard/state-dryrun/`) and records would-file findings under synthetic negative issue numbers, so repeated scans don't re-propose them and `max_open` is exercised. The ledger is `effects.jsonl` in that directory: every intended effect (issue, label, comment, commit, push, merge) is appended as one JSON line, and Gate 1 counts duplicate issues there by repeated `<!-- ard:fp=... -->` markers in `CreateIssue` bodies. Gate 1 needs only the deterministic path (CLI, config, GitHub client, effects, state, labels, executor, committer, controller, pool and the cve and stale scanners); the agent adapter, close-out and redaction are built during the soak week.\n532:| Scanner issues closed by an ard PR with no human code edits | 50% by the end of Phase 2 |\n534:| Cost per merged PR | measured in Phase 1 from the Total row of the issue's cost table; daily budget set from it |\n538:All Phase 0 decisions (#1 to #9) are recorded above.\n# ard — automated repository development\n\nard makes a GitHub repository maintain itself. Scanners file deduplicated issues, triage agents turn each issue into a plan, a worker agent implements it on a branch, reviewer agents critique the PR, and a merger lands it once every review passes.\n\n> **Status:** design stage. Nothing runs yet. The design lives in [docs/proposal.md](docs/proposal.md), and the work is tracked as [GitHub issues](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues) grouped into phase milestones.\n\n## How it works\n\nOne rule holds the system together: **a deterministic controller owns every GitHub and git side effect; agents only read and write files in a worktree.**\n\nOne `ard` process per repository runs six loops that share a small pool of job slots (default 3):\n\n| Loop | Every | What it does |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Scanners | 4 h | Find problems (CVEs, outdated components, lint, docs, missing tests…), file or update issues, close what is fixed |\n| Triage | 15 min | Role agents (lead, PM, architect, designer, doc planner, engineer) turn an issue into `plan.md` |\n| Workers | 5 min | Implement the plan on `worker/<role>-<issue#>-<slug>`, run tests, open a draft PR |\n| Reviewers | 5 min | Role agents (security, license, eng, PM) review the PR and request fixes |\n| Merger | 5 min | Squash-merge PRs whose reviews all passed and whose checks are green |\n| Reaper | 1 min | Mark dead or overdue jobs `ard:executor:failed`, keep their evidence 24 h |\n\nGitHub labels prefixed `ard:` are the queue and the state machine. `.ard/<issue#>/` in the target repository holds each issue's plan, per-role summaries and full agent logs.\n\n## Configuration by convention\n\nA target repository opts in with an `.ard/` directory. A role exists because its directory exists:\n\n```\n.ard/\n├── config.yaml          # global settings and overrides only\n├── scanners/<name>/     # scan (executable) or prompt.txt; optional Dockerfile, scripts\n├── triage/<name>/       # prompt.txt; optional Dockerfile, scripts\n├── workers/<name>/      # prompt.txt; optional Dockerfile, scripts\n├── reviewers/<name>/    # prompt.txt; optional Dockerfile, scripts\n└── <issue#>/            # per-issue output and logs, written only by ard\n```\n\nSee [Configuration](docs/proposal.md#configuration) in the proposal for the full schema and defaults.\n\n## Development\n      56 internal/config/after.go\n      40 internal/config/after_test.go\n      16 internal/config/budget_test.go\n     278 internal/config/defaults.go\n     158 internal/config/discover.go\n     153 internal/config/duration.go\n      97 internal/config/duration_test.go\n      42 internal/config/errors.go\n     491 internal/config/load.go\n     965 internal/config/load_test.go\n      88 internal/config/protected.go\n     121 internal/config/protected_test.go\n     442 internal/config/raw.go\n     182 internal/config/render.go\n      52 internal/config/render_test.go\n      24 internal/config/root.go\n      26 internal/config/select.go\n      43 internal/config/source.go\n      40 internal/config/source_test.go\n     336 internal/config/types.go\n      85 internal/config/when_test.go\n    3735 total\nengineer","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a253a109-e978-4c7d-84d8-99ab20ba9f29","uuid":"220b7018-08fb-4594-a57f-8f1c6477d24d","timestamp":"2026-11-24T12:29:53.544Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"VERSION ?= $(shell git describe --tags --always --dirty 2>/dev/null || echo dev)\nLDFLAGS := -s -w -X main.version=$(VERSION)\n\n.PHONY: build test lint fmt clean cve-fixtures lint-scanner-fixtures chart-test image-test\n\nbuild:\n\tCGO_ENABLED=0 go build -ldflags \"$(LDFLAGS)\" -o bin/ard ./cmd/ard\n\ntest:\n\tgo test ./...\n\nlint:\n\tgo vet ./...\n\t@out=\"$$(gofmt -l .)\"; if [ -n \"$$out\" ]; then echo \"gofmt needed:\"; echo \"$$out\"; exit 1; fi\n\nfmt:\n\tgofmt -w .\n\n# Renders the two Argo CD charts under deploy/ and checks them (needs helm and yq;\n# run by the ard-ci pipeline, not part of `make test`).\nchart-test:\n\tdeploy/test-charts.sh\n\n# Builds the ard image for both platforms and checks it (needs Docker buildx and\n# network; not part of `make test`).\nimage-test:\n\tdeploy/test-image.sh\n\n# Regenerates the real cve scanner fixtures inside the pinned tool image.\n# Needs Docker and network; not part of `make test`.\ncve-fixtures:\n\tdocker build -t ard-cve-fixtures internal/roles/scanners/cve\n\tdocker run --rm --user \"$$(id -u):$$(id -g)\" -e HOME=/tmp \\\n\t  -v \"$$PWD/internal/roles:/roles\" -w /roles ard-cve-fixtures sh testdata/cve/regen.sh\n\n# Regenerates the real lint scanner fixtures in the pinned image (see\n# internal/roles/testdata/lint/regen.sh). Needs Docker; not part of `make test`.\nlint-scanner-fixtures:\n\tdocker build -t ard-lint-fixtures internal/roles/scanners/lint\n\tdocker run --rm --user \"$$(id -u):$$(id -g)\" -e HOME=/tmp \\\n\t  -v \"$$PWD/internal/roles:/roles\" -w /roles ard-lint-fixtures sh testdata/lint/regen.sh\n\nclean:\n\trm -rf bin\ncmd/ard:\nmain.go\nmain_test.go\n\ndocs:\nTODO.md\nlayout.md\nproposal.md\nrunbooks\n\ninternal:\nagent\nagentjob\nbotcheck\nbudget\ncli\ncloseout\ncommitter\nconfig\nconfigcmd\nconfigsrc\ncontroller\ncostblock\ncosts\neffects\nexecutor\ngithub\ngitref\ngitx\ninitcmd\nintake\njanitor\nlabels\nlimits\nmerger\nmodelauth\npool\nprgate\nreaper\nredact\nrepopath\nreviewer\nreviewfix\nroles\nscanner\nservecmd\nstate\ntriage\nworker\nwtlock\n# ard configuration for albertcbrown/ard (dogfooding). Started from `ard init`'s\n# template; human-owned. ard serve reads it from origin/main.\n# Anything unset takes its built-in default (docs/proposal.md, Configuration).\n# Validate with `ard config check`. ard serve reads this file from origin/main.\n\n# label: only issues a maintainer labels for triage enter the pipeline.\n# all: every human-filed issue enters.\nintake: { mode: label }\n\nmerge:\n  # A PR touching any of these waits for a human approval. There is no\n  # built-in list: removing an entry removes its gate. An entry ending in /\n  # is a directory prefix; anything else is a glob (quote globs starting with *).\n  protected_paths:\n    - .github/\n    - .ard/config.yaml\n    - .ard/scanners/\n    - .ard/triage/\n    - .ard/workers/\n    - .ard/reviewers/\n    - \"**/CODEOWNERS\"\n    # Agent instructions a PR could plant to steer later agents.\n    - CLAUDE.md\n    - \"**/CLAUDE.md\"\n    - AGENTS.md\n    - \"**/AGENTS.md\"\n    - .claude/\n    - .mcp.json\n    # What builds, signs and deploys ard itself (docs/proposal.md, Delivery).\n    - \"Jenkinsfile*\"\n    - Dockerfile\n    - .dockerignore\n    - deploy/\n\noverrides:\n  scanners/cve: { every: 6h, max_open: 10, group_size: 1, autoclose: true }\n  # Phase 1 agent roles (the ard init starter set, #39 Gate 2).\n  triage/lead:  { model: claude-opus-5-5, timeout: 15m }\n  triage/engineer: { model: claude-sonnet-5-5 }\n  workers/engineer: { model: claude-sonnet-5-5, timeout: 60m, tests: make test }\n  reviewers/eng-review: { model: claude-sonnet-5-5 }\n89:**Claims need a single owner, not atomic labels.** GitHub labels are not a lock: two pollers can both add `:running`. Phase 1 runs one controller per repo behind a host lock (`flock` locally, a Kubernetes Lease in-cluster), so the controller is the only claimer. If you ever run several, the claimer re-reads the issue's label timeline and backs off unless its own `labeled` event is first.\n256:The starter engineer (`.ard/triage/engineer/prompt.txt`) plans for the worker without implementing. Its inputs are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md`, `comments.md` and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`, all untrusted. It writes the `## Implementation` section of `plan.md` with Steps, Test plan, Risk and Size, sized S, M, L or XL on a scale tied to the worker diff guard's block limit (XL is over the worker role's `diff_max_lines`, 2,000 changed lines by default). It writes `triage-engineer.md` with `role: engineer` and `attempt: 1`, never a `worker` key. It answers `needs-human` for an XL change, a change to protected role and config paths or `.github/`, or a missing product or design decision, and leaves `plan.md` untouched. In Phase 1 it has no `after:`; the lead's request orders it. The committer and close-out guards confine it to `.ard/<issue#>/`, and close-out allows it only `triage-engineer.md` and the `## Implementation` section of `plan.md`; a change to `triage-lead.md`, `issue.md` or another role's section fails the job as needs-human.\n262:Before it asks for approval or starts a worker, the gate checks that `.ard/<n>/plan.md` exists on `main`. If it does not, the gate posts one comment, adds `ard:needs-human` and records a `gate` event with reason `no-plan`; a check that fails to run is retried on the next tick. Removing a role's request label by hand does not count as that role finishing. With `require_approval: true` (the Phase 1 default) the gate posts one \"ready for approval\" comment per lead job and waits. `ard:approved` is honoured only when its latest `labeled` event (reset by an `unlabeled`) is by a collaborator with triage permission or higher, is not the App, and comes after the last triage `:done` label of the issue. An approval given earlier is left in place and ignored: the gate waits for a fresh one and does not escalate. An approval from anyone else adds `ard:needs-human` with a comment that does not name the actor. A human who adds `ard:worker:<role>` directly bypasses the gate and has approved by doing so. The gate does not enforce that `triage:engineer` ran: the lead routes and the gate applies its routing. In `--dry-run` the gate logs `triage.would_gate` and writes nothing; otherwise each action records a `gate` event.\n283:Local-executor details: the role directory (and its `Dockerfile`) is mounted from the config snapshot at the job's SHA, not from the working tree. The job worktree is always detached at a SHA resolved from the job's ref (the executor never checks out a branch; the committer owns branch worktrees). The controller's clone is a bare repository ard owns at `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` (lowercased), with no remote, no stored credential and auto-gc off (`gc.auto=0`, `maintenance.auto=false`). `ard serve` creates it on first start and seeds it with one fetch of `main` (up to 30 min; if that fails, serve starts paused and the next start retries), and later starts reuse it. Config loads and committer lanes fetch into it, and every committer and executor worktree is cut from it (lanes under `~/.ard/work/<owner>/<repo>/lanes/`). `ard serve` never writes to the operator's checkout: it reads it only for the `origin` fallback of `--repo` and the local `paused` key. To rebuild a damaged clone, stop serve, `rm -rf` it and restart. Upgrading from a version that used the checkout: ard deletes its old lane and job worktree directories without running git there, so the old `refs/ard/*` refs and the dangling worktree registrations stay in the checkout until you remove them (`git for-each-ref --format='delete %(refname)' refs/ard/ | git update-ref --stdin; git worktree prune`). Every `git worktree add`, `remove` and `prune` on the controller's clone, by the executor and the committer's lanes alike, takes one in-process lock keyed by the clone's common git directory, because git's worktree bookkeeping is not safe under concurrent admin commands; fetches, commits and pushes do not take it. Each such command also has a 30 s deadline, and cleanup runs even when the job's context is cancelled, so one hung git cannot stall every lane. Do not run `git worktree prune` (or add/remove) in `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` by hand while `ard serve` runs: the lock is per process and cannot cover a human's git. The operator's own checkout is free to use. The host keeps two files per job: `<base>.log` (stdout and stderr combined, the heartbeat source and the committed log) and `<base>.stdout` (stdout only, parsed by the adapter). A read-only job (`ReadOnly`, used by scanners) gets the worktree mounted `:ro` under Docker, or `chmod -R a-w` on the host. The heartbeat is the log file's mtime while the PID is alive; liveness alone is not a beat. Phase 0 local jobs do not survive a controller restart: the pipes belong to the controller, so after a restart the job reports `orphaned` (PID alive, start time verified) or `gone`, and the Reaper kills and fails it. The executor is not an effect: it never calls GitHub or commits, so jobs run normally under `--dry-run`. Sidecar auth: the sidecar fetch runs in the controller with the GitHub App installation token in a host-scoped `http.<url>.extraheader` passed through git's environment, never argv; the job receives plain files and no credential, and the cache stores no remote and no token. The App must be installed on the sidecar repo with contents:read. Host-mode jobs can reach `~/.ard/sidecars`, but it holds no credential. Host-mode jobs run as the controller's uid: they can read the GitHub App key and `~/.config/gh` by absolute path, write the controller's clone, and undo `chmod a-w`. Host mode is allowed for every role ([#84](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/84)), but a role that reads untrusted input (every triage, worker and reviewer role, and an LLM scanner) warns loudly when it has no `Dockerfile`; deterministic scanners are exempt, and nothing warns under `executor: k8s`, where every job runs in a container. `ard config check` and the first successful config load of `ard serve` (and any role a later config adds) log one `executor.host_untrusted` WARN per such role and also write it to stderr, so it survives `--log-level`. The local executor logs the WARN again for every host-mode job of such a role and appends a `host_untrusted` row to the `events` table. Each warning lists what the job can reach, including `~/.ard` (state, config snapshots, the pause file, other jobs' worktrees and logs). The owner accepts this risk while ard runs only on owner-controlled repos, and revisits it when moving to the spark cluster, where every job runs in a container once the k8s executor (#54) lands. Until then the Gate 1 dry-run soak on spark (see Rollout and Delivery) runs `ard serve` with the local executor in a pod, and the deterministic cve scanner runs there as a host subprocess of the controller container: its role directory has no `Dockerfile`, and the `ard` image carries its pinned tools. Only deterministic scanners run in that pod: it starts `ard serve --loops scanners,stale,reaper` ([#187](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/187)), so the triage, worker and reviewer loops never run there, even though `.ard/config.yaml` on `main` has agent roles. `--loops` takes a comma-separated list of loop names (scanners, stale, triage, workers, reviewers, reviewfix, merger, janitor, reaper, budget; `budget` is the status-issue notice loop, the claim gate is always on), defaults to every loop, and rejects an unknown name with exit 2. `ard serve` and `ard config check` refuse to run on git older than 2.47.0, because the committer's fetches fail on older versions.\n291:| Workspace | `git worktree` of the controller's clone (`~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git`) under `~/.ard/work/<owner>/<repo>/<job-id>`; sidecar exported into the job home | `Job` with an init container that shallow-clones the branch into an `emptyDir`; sidecar cloned by an init container (Phase 3) |\n296:| Good for | Phase 1, one host, fast iteration | parallelism, isolation, untrusted inputs |\n347:5. **Close-out.** The controller posts one PR review at the pinned commit (event `COMMENT`): findings on lines the PR added are inline comments, every other finding is listed in the review body. If GitHub answers 422 (a line or the commit is not in the PR), it posts the review again with every finding in the body, and if that is also refused, as a plain PR comment. It commits `review-<role>.md` and the run's redacted output to the PR branch through the per-branch committer (no `[skip ci]`: PR-branch commits always run CI so required checks report on the head), and swaps `:running` for `:done` or `:changes`. The reviewer never edits the PR. After a close-out (and on every tick, so a restart cannot miss it) the controller marks a draft worker PR ready for review once every requested review is `:done` at the PR's current head and no `:changes`, `:running`, `ard:executor:failed`, `ard:needs-human`, `ard:hold` or `ard:tests:failed` label is present; it never converts a PR back to draft and never merges, which stays with the merger (Phase 2) and humans.\n462:- **Identity: a GitHub App, not a PAT.** Installation tokens expire hourly and are scoped to contents, issues and pull requests (read and write), plus checks (read and write) and commit statuses (read). The merger reads check runs (checks: read), commit statuses (commit statuses: read) and the branch rules (metadata). The merger needs read to see required checks; the owner kept write so ard can later publish its own check runs, but nothing in Phase 1 writes checks. Leaving out the `workflows` permission makes GitHub itself reject any push that edits `.github/workflows/`, a second backstop behind a guard that already refuses all of `.github/`, and it also keeps the bot from editing the bot-commit audit. The scratch-repo run (#85) confirmed that GitHub rejects such a push (\"refusing to allow a GitHub App to create or update workflow ... without `workflows` permission\"), and that these work with exactly this permission set: label writes, PR create, review and mark-ready, pinning the status issue, and timeline and collaborator-permission reads. A personal token (`ARD_GITHUB_TOKEN`) is accepted only for local `--dry-run` development, and the client is then made read-only so no write can reach GitHub.\n509:## Rollout\n515:    P0[\"Phase 0: Skeleton<br/>controller and config<br/>label bootstrap<br/>committer, local executor<br/>cve and stale scanners<br/>dry-run mode\"] -->|\"Gate 1: 1 week dry-run, 0 duplicate issues\"| P1[\"Phase 1: First loop<br/>lead + engineer triage<br/>engineer worker<br/>eng-review<br/>Reaper<br/>human approval gate\"]\n516:    P1 -->|\"Gate 2: 10 merged PRs, failures under 10%\"| P2[\"Phase 2: All roles<br/>all triage roles<br/>all four reviewers<br/>review fix rounds<br/>merger loop<br/>remaining scanners\"]\n517:    P2 -->|\"Gate 3: 50% merged with no human code edits\"| P3[\"Phase 3: Scale<br/>k8s executor<br/>second repo<br/>shared roles repo<br/>budgets and digest\"]\n518:    P3 --> P4[\"Phase 4: Docs and site<br/>user documentation<br/>GitHub Pages site\"]\n519:    P4 --> P5[\"Phase 5: Distribution<br/>versioned releases<br/>Homebrew cask\"]\n522:Phases 4 and 5 have no gate: Phase 4 ([#178](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/178)) writes the user documentation and publishes it as a GitHub Pages site, and Phase 5 ([#177](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/177)) publishes versioned releases and a Homebrew cask (`brew install --cask albertcbrown/tap/ard`).\n524:**Where it runs (decided, #5; amended for the soak, [#144](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/144)).** `ard serve` runs live on the owner's Mac with the local executor until Phase 3, when it moves to the \"spark\" Kubernetes cluster with the k8s executor and a Lease lock in place of the `flock`. The Gate 1 soak week does not run on the Mac: it runs on spark as `ard serve --dry-run` (one pod, `Recreate`, state on a persistent volume), and the week counts from the first healthy pod; no other `ard serve` for the same repo runs meanwhile, so one ledger is counted. See Delivery. ard supports macOS and Linux only; Windows is unsupported.\n526:Phase 0 runs in dry-run: the controller logs every issue, label and commit it would make, and does none of them. Dry-run keeps its own state (`~/.ard/state-dryrun/`) and records would-file findings under synthetic negative issue numbers, so repeated scans don't re-propose them and `max_open` is exercised. The ledger is `effects.jsonl` in that directory: every intended effect (issue, label, comment, commit, push, merge) is appended as one JSON line, and Gate 1 counts duplicate issues there by repeated `<!-- ard:fp=... -->` markers in `CreateIssue` bodies. Gate 1 needs only the deterministic path (CLI, config, GitHub client, effects, state, labels, executor, committer, controller, pool and the cve and stale scanners); the agent adapter, close-out and redaction are built during the soak week.\n532:| Scanner issues closed by an ard PR with no human code edits | 50% by the end of Phase 2 |\n534:| Cost per merged PR | measured in Phase 1 from the Total row of the issue's cost table; daily budget set from it |\n538:All Phase 0 decisions (#1 to #9) are recorded above.\n# ard — automated repository development\n\nard makes a GitHub repository maintain itself. Scanners file deduplicated issues, triage agents turn each issue into a plan, a worker agent implements it on a branch, reviewer agents critique the PR, and a merger lands it once every review passes.\n\n> **Status:** design stage. Nothing runs yet. The design lives in [docs/proposal.md](docs/proposal.md), and the work is tracked as [GitHub issues](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues) grouped into phase milestones.\n\n## How it works\n\nOne rule holds the system together: **a deterministic controller owns every GitHub and git side effect; agents only read and write files in a worktree.**\n\nOne `ard` process per repository runs six loops that share a small pool of job slots (default 3):\n\n| Loop | Every | What it does |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Scanners | 4 h | Find problems (CVEs, outdated components, lint, docs, missing tests…), file or update issues, close what is fixed |\n| Triage | 15 min | Role agents (lead, PM, architect, designer, doc planner, engineer) turn an issue into `plan.md` |\n| Workers | 5 min | Implement the plan on `worker/<role>-<issue#>-<slug>`, run tests, open a draft PR |\n| Reviewers | 5 min | Role agents (security, license, eng, PM) review the PR and request fixes |\n| Merger | 5 min | Squash-merge PRs whose reviews all passed and whose checks are green |\n| Reaper | 1 min | Mark dead or overdue jobs `ard:executor:failed`, keep their evidence 24 h |\n\nGitHub labels prefixed `ard:` are the queue and the state machine. `.ard/<issue#>/` in the target repository holds each issue's plan, per-role summaries and full agent logs.\n\n## Configuration by convention\n\nA target repository opts in with an `.ard/` directory. A role exists because its directory exists:\n\n```\n.ard/\n├── config.yaml          # global settings and overrides only\n├── scanners/<name>/     # scan (executable) or prompt.txt; optional Dockerfile, scripts\n├── triage/<name>/       # prompt.txt; optional Dockerfile, scripts\n├── workers/<name>/      # prompt.txt; optional Dockerfile, scripts\n├── reviewers/<name>/    # prompt.txt; optional Dockerfile, scripts\n└── <issue#>/            # per-issue output and logs, written only by ard\n```\n\nSee [Configuration](docs/proposal.md#configuration) in the proposal for the full schema and defaults.\n\n## Development\n      56 internal/config/after.go\n      40 internal/config/after_test.go\n      16 internal/config/budget_test.go\n     278 internal/config/defaults.go\n     158 internal/config/discover.go\n     153 internal/config/duration.go\n      97 internal/config/duration_test.go\n      42 internal/config/errors.go\n     491 internal/config/load.go\n     965 internal/config/load_test.go\n      88 internal/config/protected.go\n     121 internal/config/protected_test.go\n     442 internal/config/raw.go\n     182 internal/config/render.go\n      52 internal/config/render_test.go\n      24 internal/config/root.go\n      26 internal/config/select.go\n      43 internal/config/source.go\n      40 internal/config/source_test.go\n     336 internal/config/types.go\n      85 internal/config/when_test.go\n    3735 total\nengineer","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01UknYrdC4DXy26w7gjjvdsP","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
